          body:
            "Consciousness has two parts. Arousal (wakefulness) depends on the [[reticular-activating-system|ascending reticular activating system]]: neurons in the tegmentum of the upper pons and midbrain that project, largely through the thalamus, to the whole cortex. Awareness (the content of consciousness) depends on the cerebral hemispheres.\n\nSo [[coma|coma]], an unarousable state with eyes closed and no awareness, needs one of two things: a lesion of the upper brainstem tegmentum or both thalami, or dysfunction of both hemispheres at once. Bilateral dysfunction is usually diffuse: drugs, hypoglycemia, organ failure, hypoxia, infection, or seizures, the causes of [[encephalopathy|encephalopathy]] taken to their extreme.\n\nA lesion of one hemisphere does not cause coma by itself. When a patient with a one-sided mass becomes comatose, the mass is compressing the brainstem or the other hemisphere, which is an emergency covered in the neurocritical care module. A lesion of the lower pons or medulla can be devastating but leaves the arousal system above it working.",
